Providing comprehensive care to the family is crucial in this situation. Here are the required services that must be provided by your SMA specialty care center:

Genetic counseling and education for the family to understand the diagnosis, inheritance pattern, and prognosis of spinal muscular atrophy (SMA); 
Confirmation of the SMA diagnosis through genetic testing (e.g., multiplex PCR or next-generation sequencing); 
Evaluation of the newborn's physical and neurological status, including a comprehensive physical examination and assessment of muscle strength and tone; 
Discussion of the newborn's current and future medical needs, including potential respiratory and nutritional support requirements; 
Development of a comprehensive care plan, including recommendations for home care, respiratory support, and nutritional support; 
Referral to a multidisciplinary team of healthcare professionals, including a pediatrician, pulmonologist, gastroenterologist, physical therapist, occupational therapist, and speech therapist; 
Discussion of the potential benefits and risks of available treatments, including nusinersen, onasemnogene abeparvovec, and risdiplam; 
Assessment of the family's ability to provide care and support for the newborn at home; 
Referral to a home care agency or other resources to support the family in caring for the newborn at home; 
Development of a plan for ongoing follow-up and monitoring of the newborn's condition, including regular assessments of muscle strength and respiratory function; 
Discussion of the importance of ongoing genetic counseling and education for the family; 
Referral to a support group or other resources to connect the family with other families affected by SMA; 
Development of a plan for emergency preparedness and response, including a plan for respiratory support and other medical emergencies; 
Referral to a palliative care team to provide support for the family's emotional and spiritual needs.

These services will help ensure that the family receives comprehensive care and support to manage the newborn's SMA diagnosis and provide the best possible quality of life.

Before considering a methylphenidate prescription for a 7-year-old boy with persistent ADHD symptoms, the following conditions must be met:

- A comprehensive diagnostic evaluation, including a thorough medical history, physical examination, and psychological assessment, has been conducted to confirm the diagnosis of Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD); 
- The child's symptoms have been present for at least 6 months, and they are causing significant impairment in at least two settings (e.g., home, school, or social situations); 
- The symptoms are not better explained by another mental disorder (e.g., anxiety, depression, or oppositional defiant disorder); 
- The child has not responded to behavioral interventions and classroom modifications recommended by the school counselor; 
- The child's parents have been educated about the diagnosis, treatment options, and the potential benefits and risks of medication; 
- The child's parents have provided informed consent for medication treatment; 
- A thorough medical history has been conducted to rule out any medical conditions that may be contributing to the child's symptoms (e.g., sleep disorders, vision problems, or hearing loss); 
- A physical examination has been conducted to rule out any underlying medical conditions that may be contributing to the child's symptoms; 
- The child's height, weight, and body mass index (BMI) have been recorded to monitor for any potential side effects of medication; 
- The child's family history of ADHD, substance abuse, or other mental health conditions has been assessed to inform treatment decisions; 
- The child's social and emotional functioning has been assessed to determine the need for additional support services (e.g., counseling or therapy); 
- The child's parents have been educated about the potential side effects of methylphenidate and have agreed to monitor the child's symptoms and side effects closely.

Please note that these conditions may vary depending on the specific guidelines and regulations in your region. It's always best to consult with a qualified healthcare professional for personalized advice.
